  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface high pressure fleets toward the southwest although \nupper level ridging will still impact the region and create \nsubsidence to cap us through most of today. An upper level short\nwave will ride along the periphery of the ridge and raise \nchances for shower or storm activity during this evening and \ninto Sunday morning. With the lack of strong upper level \nsteering storms that evolve may move slowly and cause issues of \nlocalized flash flooding although this would be isolated in \nnature. \n\nMuch of the area has been dry for at least a few days and the \nones that have not been dry have not received much in the way \nof heavy rainfall, therefore mainly only flood prone and low \nlying areas would be at the greatest risk for flash flooding. \nWPC has us in a slight risk for excessive rainfall along the \nOhio River and west of there. The western half of West Virignia\nis under a marginal risk for excessive rainfall as well. PWATs \nare up near 2 inches and DCAPE is sufficient to promote down \nbursting which would cause damaging wind gusts. This is \nsupported by a marginal risk for severe storms by SPC across \nmainly the Tri-state area where sufficient instability will \nexist. With the lack of shear storms will likely not last too \nlong and strong to severe ones will likely be very isolated.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAnother active day on Sunday as we will be in a marginal for\nsevere threat across much of the area for the afternoon and\nevening. WPC has us under a marginal to slight for excessive \nrainfall as well which is supportive of anomalously high PWATs \nfor this time of year. More isolated flooding is possible under\nweak steering flow and heavy downpours. The main threat from \nany strong to severe storms will be damaging wind. \n\nMore activity is then forecast as a frontal boundary drapes \nacross the region into midweek and promotes more chances of \nunsettled weather and strong to severe thunderstorm probability\nas the feature becomes quasi-stationary and meanders about the\narea slowly traveling to the south. \n\nAn upper low pressure system then digs across the Great Lakes \nand influences our area for the latter half of the work week \nwith more chances for activity under a very active weather \npattern. Temperatures will maintain below seasonable with active\nweather around and the lack of high pressure to cause any \ncompressional heating.\n\nBy end of the work week, strong high pressure looks to build in\naccording to medium range models to where the region will be\nblocked off from unsettled weather for at least the weekend.",
